    "body": "### Problem\n\nExtracted from https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41415\n\nPlugins cannot customize how dot-repeat applies their actions, so they do tricks like dynamically creating `<expr>` mappings, caching values, etc., which doesn't work well with mutlicursor cascade.\n\nExample:\n\n- replay/repeat a mapping that interacts with terminal-mode (`:FzfLua files`). currently not possible bc Nvim cannot know when the action \"ends\" (there is no count-verb-noun-etc grammar).\n    - plugins like fzf-lua could declare when the action ends, and how to repeat it.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nProvide a `setrepeat()` concept (interface/name TBD).",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "As a workaround, I've found that you can actually change the `operatorfunc` while _within_ an operator. This gives behavior somewhat like `setrepeat()`, where an operator can run arbitrary logic _only_ on dot repeat. Here's a demo:\n\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \".\", function()\n  -- Some state is invalidated when calling the operatorfunc (for example, ghk\n  -- moves all cursors up a line) See\n  -- https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41830\n  -- this mapping is only necessary if you need \"sticky cursor\"\n  vim.b.cursor_before_operator = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n  return \".\"\nend, { expr = true })\n\nlocal function repeatable_operator(keys)\n  vim.print(\"re-calling the operator\")\n  if not keys then\n    vim.b.cursor_before_operator = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n  end\n\n  -- await the end of the operator to cache the inputted keys\n  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"CmdAtom\", {\n    once = true,\n    callback = function(ev)\n      -- setup an operatorfunc specifically for dot repeat that re-calls the\n      -- operator\n      vim.o.operatorfunc = function()\n        vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, vim.b.cursor_before_operator)\n        repeatable_operator(ev.data.keys)\n      end\n    end,\n  })\n\n  -- do your normal operator logic here\n  vim.o.operatorfunc = function()\n    vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, vim.b.cursor_before_operator)\n    local range_start = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, \"[\")\n    local range_end = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_mark(0, \"]\")\n    local range = vim.range.mark(0, range_start[1], range_start[2], range_end[1], range_end[2])\n    local text = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_text(0, range:to_extmark())\n    vim.print(text)\n  end\n\n  vim.api.nvim_feedkeys(keys or \"g@\", \"ni\", false)\nend\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"gh\", function()\n  repeatable_operator()\nend)\n```\n\nThis is used here to re-call the entire function on `.`, rather than just the `operatorfunc`. We only cache the inputted `keys` here, but you could cache any state from the initial call.\n\nThe primary limitation is that we need a `.` mapping to save certain state, as the action of calling an `operatorfunc` is lossy. But if this state was accessible in `v:` marks (see #41830), this wouldn't be an issue\n\nOf course, this doesn't work in the context of `:h edit-repeat`. But it does work for vanilla usage of dot repeat.\n\nThanks to @drowning-cat for this idea - they used a version of it [here](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/discussions/41997), and I modified and simplfied it.",
