            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Could the API discussed here also support **appending a replay callback to the\ncurrent change**, particularly during Insert mode?\n\nThis also relates to [#19806](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/19806), but my\nuse case requires replaying a callback within an ongoing Insert change, rather\nthan automatically recording the resulting buffer edits.\n\nMy use case is an autopair plugin. It consumes a trigger in `InsertCharPre`, then\napplies an edit using Lua APIs outside textlock. Normal typing continues afterward.\nI need `.` to replay the plugin action *at that point within the insertion*, along\nwith the native input before and after it, preserving the original entry command\nsuch as `i`, `a`, or `ciw`.\n\nConceptually, the sequence is:\n\n```text\nnative entry command -> native input -> plugin callback -> native input -> Esc\n```\n\nI am not asking to read or arbitrarily rewrite the redo buffer, or to replace the\nwhole change with a custom `.` mapping. An append-only operation would be enough.\nFor illustration, an API might look like this (not an existing API):\n\n```lua\napply_edit_now()\nvim.redo_append(function()\n  -- Recompute the destination from the current editor state.\n  apply_edit_now()\nend)\n```\n\nThe callback would run only on replay. This could complement whole-action repeat\ncustomization: plugins would not need to reconstruct the user's surrounding\nInsert session or original change command.\n\nI tested a workaround on **Neovim 0.12.6**: write a private marker using\n`nvim_paste`, then intercept that marker through `vim.paste`. Native dot-repeat\ncalls the paste handler again, so the handler can execute Lua in between native\ninput. This demonstrates the behavior I need without remapping `.`:\n\n```lua\nlocal original_paste = vim.paste\nlocal marker = \"REDO_CALLBACK_DEMO_\" .. vim.uv.hrtime()\nlocal calls = 0\n\nvim.paste = function(lines, phase)\n  if #lines ~= 1 or lines[1] ~= marker then\n    return original_paste(lines, phase)\n  end\n\n  calls = calls + 1\n  local pos = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n  local text = \"(\" .. calls .. \")\"\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, pos[1] - 1, pos[2], pos[1] - 1, pos[2], { text })\n  vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, { pos[1], pos[2] + #text - 1 })\n  return true\nend\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"i\", \"<F5>\", function()\n  vim.api.nvim_paste(marker, false, -1)\nend)\n```\n\nWith a minimal configuration, type `i<F5>x<Esc>` to get `(1x)`. Move to another\nempty line and press `.` to get `(2x)`, then another `.` on an empty line gives\n`(3x)`. The increasing number shows that Lua is executed again, rather than just\nreusing its earlier text result.\n\nIn the autopair implementation I also tested pair resizing/deletion, close\novertyping, Enter, macros, counted dot, and undo grouping. This does not change\nnative cursor-movement boundaries: ordinary Insert arrows can split a change;\n`<C-g>U` still applies to a same-line left/right movement.\n\nThe paste workaround has drawbacks: other paste wrappers must preserve the\nhandler chain, marker IDs are process-local, and retained callbacks cannot be\nreleased accurately because plugins do not know when redo records stop referring\nto them. It also uses the paste interface to represent something other than text.\n\nA supported append-only hook could let Neovim own those references and dispatch\nthem synchronously at a safe input-loop boundary. Recording would need to be\nsuppressed during callback replay, with defined behavior for undo grouping,\ncounts and callback errors. I would be happy with an initial scope restricted to\nInsert-mode edits that modify text and cursor position.\n\nWould an append-only replay hook fit the direction of this API, or is there a\npreferred way to express this use case with CmdAtom?\n",
